{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["The reason for <b>\"and Moses took his wife and his sons\"</b> is like <i>\"and the sons of Pallu: Eliab\"</i> (Numbers 26:8), for he had only Gershom, as Scripture mentioned (Exodus 2:22), and Eliezer was conceived from him on the way or in Egypt, if she went there. And it is possible that before the prophecy that was spoken to him on the mountain of Elohim, only Gershom had been born to him, but Zipporah was pregnant, and when he returned to Jethro his father-in-law, she gave birth, and because the king's matter was urgent, he did not circumcise him and did not name him. And on the way, when his mother circumcised him, she did not name him, because Moses was being confronted by the angel. And after he went to Egypt and saw that he had been saved from all who sought his life, then he named him Eliezer, <i>\"for the Elohim of my father was my help, and delivered me from the sword of Pharaoh\"</i> (below 18:4 [n: Ramban cites verse 4, though he writes the numeral 19]). And thus our Sages mentioned that this circumcised one is Eliezer (Shemot Rabbah 5:8)." "The reason for <b>\"and he returned to the land of Egypt\"</b> is — together with those mentioned [i.e., his wife and sons]. And R. Abraham [Ibn Ezra] said (Ibn Ezra on Exodus 4:20) that <b>\"and he returned to the land of Egypt\"</b> means he alone returned, for when [the angel] encountered Moses, Eliezer was circumcised, and when he [the child] recovered, Zipporah returned with her sons to her father, and this is possible, for because Eliezer was circumcised, he [Moses] could not bring him on the way until the child grew strong, and he did not want to delay his mission for the Holy One, blessed be He. Therefore he left them at the lodging place and commanded her to return to her father's house when he [the child] recovered, and this is what is said: <i>\"after she had been sent away\"</i> (Exodus 18:2). It is also possible that they went to Egypt, and when they were delayed there, she longed for her father and he sent her away with the sons. And this is the reason for <i>\"sent her away,\"</i> for Jethro feared lest his [Moses'] intention was to divorce her. And in Shemot Rabbah (4:4): <i>\"Moses went\"</i> — where did he go? He went to take his wife and sons. Jethro said to him: Where are you taking them? To Egypt, he said. He said to him: Just as they are seeking to leave, you are bringing them to Egypt? He said to him: Tomorrow they are destined to go out and stand on Mount Sinai and hear from the mouth of the Almighty <i>\"I am YHWH your Elohim who brought you out of the land of Egypt,\"</i> and my sons will not hear with them? Jethro said to him: <i>\"Go in peace.\"</i> And according to their opinion, it is fitting that we explain [as follows]: after they [the Sages] agreed on this, the Holy One, blessed be He, commanded him that he should carry out his [Jethro's] counsel and return to Egypt with his sons and his wife, as I have explained (Ramban on Exodus 4:19)."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :register {:flags {1 ["the Almighty"]}}, :notes {0 "Ramban's verse citation reads י\"ח י\"ט; I have rendered it as he wrote it. The reference to Shemot Rabbah 5:8 follows the standard chapter numbering; some editions may vary."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:02:58.589893990Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}